From d4f27b8aa4a23979a070a8aabfb05ae81cbfed46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stefan Hertenberger <erde74@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 19:57:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 16/22] arm64: wake each new cpu until it runs (sev until it is
 in main)

mpinit starts the other processors with PSCI CPU_ON; they wait in
_start's wfe for an event, and nothing sent one, so on a QEMU that
models wfe exactly (QEMU 11) no other cpu came up and synccycles()
waited forever. A single sev() after CPU_ON (as bcm64 does) is not
enough: when it comes before the new cpu runs, it is lost. Each cpu
was then woken only by the sev for the next one, and the last one
never came up. Now mpinit sends sev() until the new cpu has set
active.machs[i] in machinit.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sys/src/9/arm64/main.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sys/src/9/arm64/main.c b/sys/src/9/arm64/main.c
index cd3b31ff1..483fa1eb5 100644
--- a/sys/src/9/arm64/main.c
+++ b/sys/src/9/arm64/main.c
@@ -145,6 +145,13 @@ mpinit(void)
 		u.r2 = PADDR(_start);
 		u.r3 = i;
 		hvccall(&u);
+		/*
+		 * the new cpu waits in _start's wfe for an event;
+		 * one sent before it runs is lost, so repeat
+		 * until it is in main.
+		 */
+		while(((volatile char*)active.machs)[i] == 0)
+			sev();
 	}
 	synccycles();
 }
